 "observations": [
  "The page number «113» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the right the number of the table, «Tab. 3», in bold sans serif, underlined. The title (L01), in bold, its note number in roman; the folios of the manuscripts, «C 43r, O 100r,» over «N 9r et 9v.», stand at the right of the title, the letters r and v raised; here as a margin note. At the foot, the signature line of the printed sheet: «D. K. D. Vidensk. Selsk. Skr., 7 Række, hist. og filos. Afd. III. 1.», here «7 Række» without the point after the 7, and the sheet number «15» on the right; recorded here, not transcribed as a line.",
  "The table: a thick and a thin rule above the head, a double rule below it; vertical rules, some of them double, divide the fourteen columns; short rules under the heads that span several columns; no rule closes the table at the foot. The heads of the first, the sixth and the last column, «Anni collecti arabum», «Anni plani arabum» and «Anni Roma-norum per 28 dividendi», are printed turned through a quarter, reading upwards, in two or three lines; here they stand in the first row (L02). Each printed row is one line here, the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. The Arab months stand on every second row, the multiples of 28 on every row from the second.",
  "L05-L28, the collected years. The years of Alexander are counted here in years of 365 1/4 days, «secundum bissestum», of twelve months of 30 days and 5 1/4 added days (the «Partes» are quarters of a day); Suter explains the reckoning with examples on pp. 38-39 (JP2 0070-0071). Checked: the radix, 932 years 9 months 17 days, is 287 days after the beginning of the year 933 of Alexander on 1 October 621, that is Thursday 15 July 622, the epoch of the tables (p. 39, JP2 0071). Each row adds 30 Arab years, 10 631 days, that is 29 years 1 month 8 3/4 days: the rows from the radix to 600 agree to the quarter day (with the readings of notes b and c); 30 years carry over into the next year as 12 months and 5 1/4 days, as at 90 and 360.",
  "L26-L28, the rows 630, 660 and 690, given by C alone (note a). Checked: they are each 4 1/4 days too large; 10 631 days added to the row 600, 1514 years 11 months 1 1/2 days, give 1544 years 0 months 5 days, not 9 1/4, and the rows 660 and 690 continue from 9 1/4 by the same step (computed 1573 y 1 m 13 3/4 d and 1602 y 2 m 22 1/2 d). The edition prints the values of C; this is no misprint of the edition, and no critical note is made.",
  "L05-L34, the single years. Checked: the row p gives the days of the first p Arab years, 354 days and 355 in the leap years 2, 5, 7, 10, 13, 16, 18, 21, 24, 26 and 29, in years of 365 1/4 days, months of 30 days and days: all 30 rows as printed (with the reading of note d), the last, 29 years 1 month 8 3/4 days, the 10 631 days of a cycle. L06-L28, «Menses omnes». Checked: the months of 30 and 29 days in turn give 30, 59, 89, … 354 days, 1 month 0 days to 11 months 24 days, all twelve as printed. The last column holds the multiples of 28 from 1400 to 2016, as in Tab. 1 (p. 109, JP2 0141).",
  "N02: «[recte: ante eram]», the editor's correction in italics within square brackets, the colon set close. N03: «Alterutre temporum regum extractiones (?)», the superscription of C and N, with the editor's question mark; the semicolon after «CN» and the colon after the second «CN» stand after the thin space of the edition (9 px). N07: «Jum. el’.», «Ra’ham» (N08), with the apostrophe. N10: «a-a)», the two places of note a, the rows 630 to 690. Italics: «om.», «recte: ante eram», «suprascr.», «Hanc column. om.»."
 ],
